```Definition of Associative Field
The associative field is a linguistic concept defined as the network of words related to a common theme or aspect of reality, where connections are based on subjective associations rather than strict grammatical or etymological rules.
 It was first introduced by Swiss linguist Charles Bally in 1940, who likened words to constellations, each word acting as a central point around which related terms converge.
 Unlike semantic fields, which have a structured, centripetal organization based on definable semantic differences, associative fields are considered to have a centrifugal nature, extending without fixed boundaries and varying significantly between individuals, social groups, and contexts.
 Examples include the word "bed," which may evoke associations such as sleep, sheets, pillows, or relaxation, depending on personal experience.
